/* PaKuWord – English daily pack 2026-11-27: new domain Astronomy (30) + 30 entries in existing domains (his, mat, arc, cook, art) */
window.PW_CONTENT = window.PW_CONTENT || {};
window.PW_CONTENT.en = (window.PW_CONTENT.en || []).concat([
/* ---------- ASTRONOMY ---------- */
{id:"ast_astronomy",cat:"ast",t:"Astronomy",
 s:"The science that studies stars, planets, galaxies and everything else beyond the Earth's atmosphere.",
 l:"Astronomers observe the sky with their eyes, telescopes, radio dishes and space probes, then use physics and mathematics to work out what objects are made of, how far away they are and how they change. Astronomy is one of the oldest sciences, because people have always used the Sun, Moon and stars to keep calendars and find their way. It is not the same as astrology, which claims that the positions of planets shape people's personalities and futures; that claim is not supported by scientific evidence.",
 x:"She joined the astronomy club at school and now knows how to find Saturn in the evening sky.",
 f:"The word comes from two Greek words: astron, meaning star, and nomos, meaning law or arrangement.",
 js:"The science of stars, planets and space.",
 jl:"Astronomy is the study of everything up in the sky and far beyond it: the Sun, the Moon, planets, stars and huge groups of stars. Astronomers use telescopes and clever maths to find out how big things are, how far away they are and what they are made of.",
 jx:"For his astronomy project, Leo drew the Moon every night for a whole month."},
{id:"ast_planet",cat:"ast",t:"Planet",
 s:"A large, round body that travels around a star and has cleared other objects out of its path.",
 l:"Our Solar System has eight planets. The four closest to the Sun, Mercury, Venus, Earth and Mars, are small and rocky. The four outer ones, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus and Neptune, are much larger and made mostly of gas or ice. A planet does not make its own light like a star; we see planets because they reflect sunlight. That is why, through a telescope, Venus shows phases just like the Moon.",
 x:"The bright dot near the Moon tonight is not a star but the planet Jupiter.",
 f:"Planet comes from a Greek word meaning wanderer, because ancient sky watchers noticed that a few bright points slowly moved against the fixed pattern of the stars.",
 js:"A big round world that travels around a star.",
 jl:"The Earth is a planet, and so are Mars, Jupiter and five others. They all travel around the Sun in big loops. Planets do not shine by themselves. They look bright because the Sun's light bounces off them, like light off a mirror.",
 jx:"Zoe remembered the order of the planets with a funny sentence her teacher taught her."},
{id:"ast_dwarf_planet",cat:"ast",t:"Dwarf planet",
 s:"A round body that orbits the Sun but has not cleared its neighbourhood of other objects, so it is not counted as a full planet.",
 l:"In 2006 the International Astronomical Union agreed on a definition: a planet orbits the Sun, is massive enough for its own gravity to pull it into a round shape, and has cleared its orbit of other similar objects. A dwarf planet passes the first two tests but not the third, because it shares its region with many other bodies. Pluto, which moves among countless icy objects beyond Neptune, became a dwarf planet under this rule, together with Ceres in the asteroid belt and Eris.",
 x:"My grandad still calls Pluto the ninth planet, but my textbook calls it a dwarf planet.",
 f:"Ceres was discovered in 1801 by the Italian astronomer Giuseppe Piazzi and was first called a planet, then an asteroid, and since 2006 a dwarf planet.",
 js:"A small round world that is not quite a full planet.",
 jl:"Pluto is round and travels around the Sun, just like a planet. But it lives in a crowded area full of other icy rocks. Scientists decided that a real planet has to be the boss of its own path, so Pluto is now called a dwarf planet.",
 jx:"Our class voted on whether Pluto should be a planet, and the dwarf planet side won."},
{id:"ast_exoplanet",cat:"ast",t:"Exoplanet",
 s:"A planet that orbits a star other than the Sun.",
 l:"Exoplanets are so far away and so faint next to their stars that they are rarely seen directly. Astronomers usually find them indirectly: a star dims slightly when a planet passes in front of it, or it wobbles a little as the planet's gravity tugs on it. Thousands of exoplanets are now known. Some are giant gas planets orbiting very close to their stars, and some are rocky worlds in the zone where liquid water could exist, which makes them interesting in the search for life.",
 x:"The space telescope measured the light of the star to learn what the exoplanet's air is made of.",
 f:"The first confirmed planets outside the Solar System were announced in 1992 by Aleksander Wolszczan and Dale Frail, orbiting a dead, spinning star called a pulsar; in 1995 Michel Mayor and Didier Queloz found the first one around a star like the Sun.",
 js:"A planet that goes around a faraway star, not our Sun.",
 jl:"Our Sun is a star, and the night sky is full of other stars. Many of them have their own planets too. These faraway planets are called exoplanets. They are too far to visit, but scientists can spot them because their star flickers a tiny bit when a planet passes in front.",
 jx:"Ben imagined what it would be like to watch two sunsets on an exoplanet with two suns."},
{id:"ast_orbit",cat:"ast",t:"Orbit",
 s:"The curved path that one object in space follows around another because of gravity.",
 l:"The Moon orbits the Earth, and the Earth orbits the Sun. An object in orbit is moving sideways very fast while gravity keeps pulling it inwards, so instead of flying away or falling straight down it keeps falling around the bigger body. Orbits are not perfect circles but slightly stretched ovals called ellipses. The time an object takes to go around once is its orbital period: for the Earth around the Sun, that is one year.",
 x:"The satellite needs about ninety minutes to complete one orbit of the Earth.",
 f:"In the early seventeenth century Johannes Kepler showed that planets move in ellipses, not circles, and that they travel faster when they are closer to the Sun.",
 js:"The path something takes as it goes round and round something else in space.",
 jl:"Tie a ball to a string and swing it round your head. The string keeps pulling the ball in, so it goes in a circle. In space there is no string, but gravity does the same job. That is why the Moon keeps going around the Earth. Its path is called an orbit.",
 jx:"On the poster, a dotted line shows the orbit of each planet around the Sun."},
{id:"ast_light_year",cat:"ast",t:"Light-year",
 s:"The distance that light travels in one year, used to measure huge distances in space.",
 l:"Light is the fastest thing there is, moving at almost three hundred thousand kilometres every second. In a year it covers about nine and a half trillion kilometres, and that distance is one light-year. Despite its name, a light-year measures distance, not time. Because light takes time to reach us, looking at distant objects means looking into the past: we see a star as it was when its light set off.",
 x:"The nearest star to the Sun is a little over four light-years away.",
 f:"Sunlight takes about eight minutes to reach the Earth, so if the Sun suddenly vanished, we would not notice for about eight minutes.",
 js:"How far light travels in one whole year.",
 jl:"Light is super fast. It could go around the Earth more than seven times in one second. Now imagine how far it travels in a whole year! That giant distance is called a light-year. Scientists use it because stars are much too far away to measure in kilometres.",
 jx:"Mia was amazed that the star she was looking at sent its light before she was born."},
{id:"ast_constellation",cat:"ast",t:"Constellation",
 s:"A group of stars that people have imagined as a picture, and the area of the sky around it.",
 l:"Long ago, people joined bright stars with imaginary lines to make figures such as a hunter, a lion or a swan, and told stories about them. Today astronomers divide the whole sky into eighty-eight official constellations with fixed borders, a bit like countries on a map, so that every star belongs to exactly one. The stars in a constellation only look close together from the Earth; in reality some may be much farther away than others.",
 x:"Orion is one of the easiest constellations to find on a winter evening.",
 f:"Many constellation names used today come from a list of forty-eight constellations described by the Greek astronomer Ptolemy in the second century.",
 js:"A picture made by joining up stars in the sky.",
 jl:"If you look at the stars like dot-to-dot puzzles, you can see shapes: a hunter, a big bear or a swan. People made up these shapes thousands of years ago and told stories about them. Each shape is called a constellation.",
 jx:"Grandpa showed me the constellation of the Swan, flying along the Milky Way."},
{id:"ast_galaxy",cat:"ast",t:"Galaxy",
 s:"An enormous system of stars, gas and dust held together by gravity.",
 l:"Galaxies can contain millions or even hundreds of billions of stars. Some are flat spirals with curving arms, some are smooth oval shapes, and some are irregular clouds. Our own galaxy, the Milky Way, is a spiral. Galaxies are separated by vast, almost empty distances, and they often gather in groups and clusters. Most of them are so far away that they look like faint smudges, even through a good telescope.",
 x:"On a dark autumn night you can see the Andromeda Galaxy as a faint, fuzzy patch without a telescope.",
 f:"The word galaxy comes from the Greek word for milk, which is also why our own galaxy is called the Milky Way.",
 js:"A giant family of billions of stars in space.",
 jl:"Stars are not spread out evenly in space. They live together in huge groups called galaxies. Our Sun belongs to a galaxy called the Milky Way, which has billions of stars. Out in space there are countless other galaxies too.",
 jx:"Ella painted a swirling purple galaxy on her bedroom wall."},
{id:"ast_milky_way",cat:"ast",t:"Milky Way",
 s:"The spiral galaxy that contains our Sun, seen from the Earth as a pale band of light across the night sky.",
 l:"We live inside the Milky Way, in a quiet area roughly halfway between the centre and the edge. Because we see the galaxy's disc from within, it looks like a long, cloudy river of light. A telescope shows that this glow is made of countless stars too faint to see one by one. At the centre of the Milky Way there is a very heavy black hole. City lights hide the Milky Way, so many people have never seen it.",
 x:"Away from the city, we lay on the grass and watched the Milky Way stretch over the lake.",
 f:"The Sun takes roughly two hundred and thirty million years to travel once around the centre of the Milky Way.",
 js:"Our home galaxy, which looks like a milky stripe across the night sky.",
 jl:"On a very dark night, far away from street lights, you can see a pale, cloudy stripe across the sky. That stripe is made of millions of stars. It is our own galaxy, the Milky Way, and our Sun is one of its stars.",
 jx:"At the campsite there were no lamps, so we could finally see the Milky Way."},
{id:"ast_nebula",cat:"ast",t:"Nebula",
 s:"A huge cloud of gas and dust in space, where new stars can form or which a dying star has thrown off.",
 l:"Some nebulae glow brightly because hot young stars inside them make the gas shine. Others are dark and block the light of stars behind them. Inside dense nebulae, gravity slowly pulls gas together until new stars are born, so they are sometimes called star nurseries. Other nebulae are the expanding remains of old stars. The colourful space pictures we see online are often nebulae photographed through special filters over many hours.",
 x:"The Orion Nebula is visible as a small misty patch in Orion's sword.",
 f:"Nebula is the Latin word for mist or cloud.",
 js:"A giant colourful cloud in space where stars are born.",
 jl:"A nebula is a gigantic cloud of gas and dust floating in space. In some nebulae, the gas slowly squeezes together and new stars light up. That is why scientists call them star nurseries. Photos of nebulae are some of the most beautiful pictures of space.",
 jx:"Ollie's favourite poster shows a pink and blue nebula full of baby stars."},
{id:"ast_supernova",cat:"ast",t:"Supernova",
 s:"A gigantic explosion of a star, which can briefly shine brighter than a whole galaxy.",
 l:"A supernova happens when a very heavy star runs out of fuel and its core collapses, or when a small dead star called a white dwarf takes in too much material from a neighbour. The explosion scatters the star's outer layers into space. These clouds carry elements made inside stars, such as oxygen, calcium and iron, which later become part of new stars and planets. The iron in your blood was once made inside stars and spread by explosions like these.",
 x:"Astronomers spotted a new point of light in a distant galaxy and confirmed it was a supernova.",
 f:"In 1054 Chinese astronomers recorded a new star so bright it could be seen in daylight for weeks; its remains are the Crab Nebula.",
 js:"The super-bright explosion of a giant star.",
 jl:"Very big stars do not fade away quietly. When they run out of fuel, they explode in a blast so bright it can outshine billions of other stars. This is a supernova. The explosion throws star stuff into space, and some of it ends up in new planets, and even in people.",
 jx:"Our teacher said that the iron in our bodies came from a supernova long, long ago."},
{id:"ast_black_hole",cat:"ast",t:"Black hole",
 s:"A region of space where matter is packed so tightly that its gravity lets nothing escape, not even light.",
 l:"A black hole can form when a very heavy star collapses at the end of its life. Its boundary is called the event horizon: anything that crosses it cannot get out again. Black holes cannot be seen directly, but astronomers detect them by the way they pull on nearby stars and by the hot, glowing gas that swirls around them. Giant black holes, millions or billions of times heavier than the Sun, sit at the centres of most large galaxies, including our own.",
 x:"The documentary explained how a black hole bends the light of stars behind it.",
 f:"In 2019 the Event Horizon Telescope, a network of radio telescopes around the world, released the first image of the shadow of a black hole, in the galaxy M87.",
 js:"A place in space that pulls so hard that even light can't get out.",
 jl:"A black hole is what can be left when a giant star squashes itself into a tiny space. Its pull is so strong that nothing that gets too close can escape, not even light, so it looks completely black. Don't worry: there are no black holes anywhere near the Earth.",
 jx:"Sam made a model of a black hole with a funnel and swirling glitter."},
{id:"ast_pulsar",cat:"ast",t:"Pulsar",
 s:"A rapidly spinning dead star that sends out beams of radio waves, seen from Earth as regular pulses.",
 l:"When a heavy star explodes, its core can be squeezed into a neutron star: a ball only about twenty kilometres across but heavier than the Sun. Some neutron stars spin many times per second and shoot beams of radiation from their magnetic poles. If a beam sweeps across the Earth, we detect a pulse at every turn, like the flash of a lighthouse. Pulsars tick so steadily that astronomers use them as extremely precise clocks.",
 x:"The radio telescope picked up the pulsar's beat, steady as a metronome.",
 f:"The first pulsar was discovered in 1967 by Jocelyn Bell Burnell, a research student in Cambridge, who noticed a strange regular signal in her radio data.",
 js:"A tiny spinning star that flashes like a lighthouse.",
 jl:"When a giant star explodes, it can leave behind a small, super-heavy ball that spins round and round very fast. As it spins, it sends out beams, like a lighthouse. Each time a beam points at the Earth, our telescopes notice a little blip. That spinning star is a pulsar.",
 jx:"We listened to a recording of a pulsar, and it sounded like a ticking clock."},
{id:"ast_comet",cat:"ast",t:"Comet",
 s:"A small icy body that grows a glowing head and long tails when its orbit brings it close to the Sun.",
 l:"A comet's solid centre, the nucleus, is a lump of ice, dust and rock, often only a few kilometres across. Far from the Sun it is frozen and dark. As it approaches the Sun, the ice turns into gas, which bursts out with dust and forms a fuzzy cloud called the coma. The sunlight and the flow of particles from the Sun push this material into tails that always point away from the Sun, whichever way the comet is moving.",
 x:"In the photo, the comet's tail stretches across the sky above the mountains.",
 f:"Halley's Comet returns roughly every seventy-five to seventy-six years; it was last seen in 1986 and is expected back in 2061.",
 js:"A big dirty snowball in space that grows a shining tail near the Sun.",
 jl:"A comet is like a giant snowball mixed with dust and rock. It travels around the Sun on a very long path. When it comes near the Sun, it warms up, and gas and dust stream off it. That makes a glowing tail that can stretch for millions of kilometres.",
 jx:"Grandma saw Halley's Comet when she was young, and I hope to see it when I'm older."},
{id:"ast_meteor",cat:"ast",t:"Meteor",
 s:"A streak of light in the sky made when a small piece of space rock or dust burns up in the Earth's atmosphere.",
 l:"Three similar words are easy to mix up. A meteoroid is a small rock or speck of dust travelling through space. When it enters the atmosphere at high speed, the air in front of it gets so hot that it glows, and that flash of light is a meteor, often called a shooting star. If a piece survives and lands on the ground, it is a meteorite. Meteor showers happen when the Earth passes through the trail of dust left behind by a comet.",
 x:"We stayed up late in August and counted twenty meteors in one hour.",
 f:"The Perseid meteor shower, which peaks around the middle of August each year, comes from dust left by a comet called Swift–Tuttle.",
 js:"A shooting star: a bit of space dust burning up in the sky.",
 jl:"A shooting star is not really a star. It is a tiny piece of space rock, often smaller than a pea, that zooms into the air around the Earth. It goes so fast that it heats up and glows, leaving a bright line in the sky for a second. If a piece lands on the ground, we call it a meteorite.",
 jx:"Lily saw a meteor flash across the sky and quickly made a wish."},
{id:"ast_asteroid",cat:"ast",t:"Asteroid",
 s:"A rocky or metallic object, smaller than a planet, that orbits the Sun.",
 l:"Most asteroids in our Solar System travel in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter. They range from boulders to bodies hundreds of kilometres wide, and most are irregular in shape, like potatoes. Asteroids are leftovers from the time when the planets formed, so studying them helps scientists understand the early Solar System. Astronomers track asteroids that come near the Earth, and in 2022 the DART spacecraft deliberately crashed into one to test whether its path could be changed.",
 x:"The space probe collected a tiny sample of dust from the surface of an asteroid.",
 f:"Despite what films show, the asteroid belt is mostly empty space; spacecraft fly through it without needing to dodge rocks.",
 js:"A big space rock that travels around the Sun.",
 jl:"Asteroids are rocks left over from when the planets were made, a very long time ago. Some are as small as a car, and some are as big as a country. Most of them circle the Sun in a wide ring between Mars and Jupiter, called the asteroid belt.",
 jx:"In our game, the spaceship had to fly safely past an asteroid."},
{id:"ast_eclipse",cat:"ast",t:"Eclipse",
 s:"An event in which one body in space blocks the light reaching another, as when the Moon hides the Sun.",
 l:"In a solar eclipse, the Moon passes between the Sun and the Earth and covers part or all of the Sun for people in a narrow area. During a total solar eclipse the sky darkens and the Sun's pale outer atmosphere, the corona, becomes visible. In a lunar eclipse, the Earth's shadow falls on the Moon, which often turns a coppery red. It is never safe to look at the Sun directly, even when it is partly covered; special eclipse glasses or indirect methods are needed.",
 x:"We watched the lunar eclipse from the balcony as the Moon slowly turned red.",
 f:"Total solar eclipses are possible because the Sun is about four hundred times wider than the Moon but also about four hundred times farther away, so both look almost the same size in our sky.",
 js:"When the Moon covers the Sun, or the Earth's shadow covers the Moon.",
 jl:"Sometimes the Sun, the Earth and the Moon line up. If the Moon gets between us and the Sun, it hides the Sun for a few minutes and the day goes dark. That is a solar eclipse. If the Earth's shadow falls on the Moon, the Moon can glow red. That is a lunar eclipse. Never look straight at the Sun!",
 jx:"We made a pinhole viewer from a cereal box to watch the eclipse safely."},
{id:"ast_moon_phases",cat:"ast",t:"Phases of the Moon",
 s:"The changing shapes of the lit part of the Moon that we see from the Earth during a month.",
 l:"The Moon does not shine by itself; half of it is always lit by the Sun. As it travels around the Earth, we see different amounts of that sunlit half. At new moon the lit side faces away from us and the Moon is almost invisible. Then it grows, or waxes, through a crescent and a half moon to a full moon, and afterwards shrinks, or wanes, again. A whole cycle takes about twenty-nine and a half days. The phases are not caused by the Earth's shadow.",
 x:"The calendar on our fridge shows the phases of the Moon for every day of the month.",
 f:"The English words month and Monday are both related to the word moon, because people have long measured time by the Moon's cycle.",
 js:"The different shapes the Moon seems to have during a month.",
 jl:"Sometimes the Moon looks like a thin smile, sometimes like half a cookie, and sometimes like a full round plate. The Moon does not really change shape. We just see more or less of its sunny side as it travels around the Earth. These shapes are called the phases of the Moon.",
 jx:"Amir drew a Moon diary and saw the crescent grow into a full moon."},
{id:"ast_equinox",cat:"ast",t:"Equinox",
 s:"One of the two days each year, in March and September, when day and night are roughly equal in length all over the world.",
 l:"The Earth is tilted on its axis. At an equinox, neither the northern nor the southern half is tipped towards the Sun, so the Sun rises almost exactly in the east and sets almost exactly in the west. The March equinox, around the twentieth of March, marks the start of astronomical spring in the north and autumn in the south. The September equinox, around the twenty-second or twenty-third, does the opposite. Day and night are close to equal, but because of the way sunrise is measured the day is usually a few minutes longer.",
 x:"On the equinox, we measured our shadows at noon and compared them with June.",
 f:"Equinox comes from Latin words meaning equal night.",
 js:"A day in spring or autumn when day and night are about the same length.",
 jl:"Twice a year, once in March and once in September, the day and the night are almost exactly the same length, about twelve hours each. These days are called equinoxes. After the March one, the days in our part of the world get longer than the nights.",
 jx:"On the spring equinox, our class planted sunflower seeds."},
{id:"ast_solstice",cat:"ast",t:"Solstice",
 s:"The day of the year with the longest or the shortest daylight, in June and December.",
 l:"Because the Earth's axis is tilted, the Sun's midday height changes through the year. At the June solstice, around the twenty-first of June, the northern hemisphere leans most towards the Sun, giving its longest day and shortest night, while the southern hemisphere has its shortest day. At the December solstice, around the twenty-first or twenty-second of December, it is the other way round. After the winter solstice the days slowly start getting longer again, which many cultures have celebrated.",
 x:"On the summer solstice it was still light outside when we went to bed.",
 f:"Solstice comes from Latin words meaning the Sun stands still, because around these dates the Sun's midday height seems to stop changing before it turns back.",
 js:"The longest day or the shortest day of the year.",
 jl:"In June there is one day when the Sun stays up the longest, and in December there is a day when it goes down the earliest. These special days are the solstices. After the shortest day in December, every day gets a little bit lighter again.",
 jx:"We had a picnic dinner on the solstice and watched the very late sunset."},
{id:"ast_telescope",cat:"ast",t:"Telescope",
 s:"An instrument that collects light or other radiation from faraway objects to make them look brighter, bigger and clearer.",
 l:"A refracting telescope uses glass lenses to bend and focus light, while a reflecting telescope uses curved mirrors. The most important part is the size of the main lens or mirror: the bigger it is, the more light it gathers and the fainter the objects it can show. Some telescopes do not use visible light at all. Radio telescopes are giant dishes, and space telescopes orbit above the atmosphere, which blurs and blocks some light, to see infrared, ultraviolet or X-rays.",
 x:"Through the telescope, we could see the rings of Saturn as a tiny, perfect oval.",
 f:"Isaac Newton built one of the first working reflecting telescopes around 1668, using a curved metal mirror instead of a lens.",
 js:"A tube with lenses or mirrors that makes faraway things look closer.",
 jl:"A telescope is like a giant eye that collects lots of light. It makes the Moon's craters, Saturn's rings and faraway stars much easier to see. Some telescopes are so big that they need their own building, and some even float in space.",
 jx:"Grandad set up his telescope in the garden so we could look at the Moon's craters."},
{id:"ast_space_station",cat:"ast",t:"International Space Station",
 s:"A large laboratory that orbits the Earth, where astronauts from several countries live and do experiments.",
 l:"The International Space Station, often shortened to the ISS, was built in orbit piece by piece from 1998 by space agencies including those of the United States, Russia, Europe, Japan and Canada. It circles the Earth about sixteen times a day at a height of around four hundred kilometres. The crew study how weightlessness affects the human body, plants and materials, which helps prepare for longer journeys. From the ground it can be seen as a bright, steady dot moving quickly across the sky.",
 x:"We checked a website to see when the space station would fly over our town.",
 f:"People have lived on board the space station continuously since November 2000.",
 js:"A big flying science lab where astronauts live, high above the Earth.",
 jl:"High above the clouds, a huge station flies around the Earth. Astronauts from different countries live there for months. They float around, sleep in sleeping bags fixed to the walls and do science experiments. On a clear evening you can sometimes see the station as a bright moving dot.",
 jx:"Noah waved at the space station as it flew over our house."},
{id:"ast_aurora",cat:"ast",t:"Aurora (northern lights)",
 s:"Moving curtains of coloured light in the night sky, caused by particles from the Sun hitting the upper atmosphere.",
 l:"The Sun constantly sends out a stream of charged particles called the solar wind. The Earth's magnetic field guides some of them towards the polar regions, where they collide with oxygen and nitrogen high in the atmosphere and make them glow, mostly green, sometimes red or purple. In the north this is called the aurora borealis, or northern lights, and in the south the aurora australis. After strong eruptions on the Sun, auroras can be seen much farther from the poles than usual.",
 x:"In Norway we stood in the snow and watched green lights dance above the fjord.",
 f:"Aurora was the Roman goddess of the dawn, borealis means northern and australis means southern.",
 js:"Beautiful glowing colours that dance in the sky near the North and South Poles.",
 jl:"Far in the north and the south, the night sky sometimes fills with waving ribbons of green and pink light. This happens when tiny bits from the Sun crash into the air high above the Earth and make it glow. People call it the northern lights, or the aurora.",
 jx:"Freya's dream is to travel to Iceland and see the aurora."},
{id:"ast_light_pollution",cat:"ast",t:"Light pollution",
 s:"Too much artificial light at night, which brightens the sky and hides the stars.",
 l:"Street lamps, advertising screens and lit buildings send a lot of light upwards and sideways, where it is not needed. This makes the night sky glow orange or grey over towns and hides all but the brightest stars. Light pollution also confuses animals: migrating birds, moths and newly hatched turtles can be drawn towards lights. It wastes energy too. Simple fixes include shielded lamps that point downwards, warmer colours and switching off lights that nobody is using.",
 x:"Because of light pollution, we could only see a handful of stars from our city flat.",
 f:"Some places, such as parts of national parks, are officially recognised as dark-sky areas, where lighting is controlled so that people can still see the Milky Way.",
 js:"When there is so much light at night that we can't see the stars.",
 jl:"In big cities there are lamps, shop signs and windows glowing all night. All this light shines up into the sky and hides the stars. It can also confuse animals that need the dark, like moths, bats and owls. This problem is called light pollution.",
 jx:"Our school switched off the playground lights at night to cut light pollution."},
{id:"ast_big_bang",cat:"ast",t:"Big Bang",
 s:"The scientific theory that the universe began about thirteen point eight billion years ago in an extremely hot, dense state and has been expanding ever since.",
 l:"The Big Bang theory does not describe an explosion in one place, but the whole of space stretching out from a very hot and crowded beginning. As the universe expanded and cooled, the first atoms formed, then stars and galaxies. The theory is supported by several kinds of evidence: galaxies are moving apart, the whole sky glows faintly with leftover heat called the cosmic microwave background, and the amounts of the lightest elements match the predictions.",
 x:"In our science lesson we made a timeline of the universe, starting with the Big Bang.",
 f:"The name Big Bang was used by the astronomer Fred Hoyle in a BBC radio talk in 1949; he did not believe in the theory himself.",
 js:"The scientific idea of how the universe began, a very, very long time ago.",
 jl:"Scientists think that long, long ago, everything in the universe was squeezed together, extremely hot. Then it began to spread out, and it is still spreading today. Slowly, stars, galaxies and planets formed. Scientists call this beginning the Big Bang.",
 jx:"We blew up a balloon with dots on it to see how galaxies move apart after the Big Bang."},
{id:"ast_redshift",cat:"ast",t:"Redshift",
 s:"The stretching of light from an object moving away from us, which shifts its colours towards the red end of the spectrum.",
 l:"When a source of light moves away, its light waves are stretched out, so they become longer and redder. A similar thing happens with sound: an ambulance siren sounds lower as it drives away. By splitting a galaxy's light into a spectrum, astronomers can measure how far its patterns have shifted. Almost all distant galaxies show redshift, and the farther away they are, the bigger it is. This is one of the main pieces of evidence that the universe is expanding.",
 x:"The redshift of the galaxy told the team it was billions of light-years away.",
 f:"Georges Lemaître proposed in 1927 that the universe is expanding, and Edwin Hubble published observations in 1929; in 2018 astronomers recommended calling the relationship the Hubble–Lemaître law.",
 js:"How light from things moving away from us looks a bit redder.",
 jl:"Have you noticed that a siren sounds lower when the ambulance drives away? Light does something similar. When a galaxy moves away from us, its light looks a little more red. Scientists use this to work out that galaxies are rushing apart.",
 jx:"Our teacher used a slinky spring to show how waves stretch in redshift."},
{id:"ast_en_plough",cat:"ast",only:1,t:"The Plough",
 s:"The British name for seven bright stars in the constellation of the Great Bear, called the Big Dipper in North America.",
 l:"The Plough is not a constellation itself but a striking pattern, called an asterism, within Ursa Major, the Great Bear. Its seven stars form a shape like an old horse-drawn plough or a long-handled saucepan. From Britain it never sets, so it can be seen on every clear night of the year. The two stars at the end of the bowl are known as the Pointers: following the line between them upwards leads to Polaris, the North Star.",
 x:"Dad taught me to use the Plough to find the North Star on our camping trip.",
 f:"The same seven stars have many names around the world: Charles's Wain in old English folklore, the Big Dipper in the United States, and the Great Cart or Wagon in many European languages.",
 js:"Seven bright stars that make the shape of a plough or a saucepan.",
 jl:"If you look north on a clear night in Britain, you can find seven bright stars shaped like a saucepan with a long handle. People here call it the Plough. Two of its stars point the way to the North Star, which helped travellers find their way.",
 jx:"Ruby found the Plough first and then followed the Pointers to the North Star."},
{id:"ast_en_greenwich",cat:"ast",only:1,t:"Royal Observatory, Greenwich",
 s:"A historic observatory in south-east London, home of the Prime Meridian and Greenwich Mean Time.",
 l:"King Charles the Second founded the Royal Observatory in 1675 to improve navigation at sea by mapping the stars accurately. The astronomers there, led by the Astronomer Royal, timed stars crossing a north-south line through the observatory. In 1884 an international conference chose this line as the Prime Meridian, longitude zero, and Greenwich Mean Time became the basis of the world's time zones. Today the site is a museum where visitors can stand with one foot in each hemisphere.",
 x:"On our London trip we took a photo standing on the Prime Meridian at Greenwich.",
 f:"A red time ball on top of the observatory has dropped at one o'clock almost every afternoon since 1833, so that ships on the River Thames could set their clocks.",
 js:"A famous old star-watching building in London, where the world's time zones start.",
 jl:"In Greenwich, in London, there is an old observatory on a hill. A metal line on the ground there marks longitude zero, an imaginary line from the North Pole to the South Pole. You can stand with one foot on the east side of the world and one on the west!",
 jx:"At Greenwich, Max stood with one foot in the east and one in the west."},
{id:"ast_en_jodrell_bank",cat:"ast",only:1,t:"Jodrell Bank",
 s:"An observatory in Cheshire famous for the giant Lovell Telescope, one of the largest steerable radio telescopes in the world.",
 l:"Jodrell Bank was started after the Second World War by the astronomer Bernard Lovell, who used army radar equipment to study meteors. The great dish now named after him, seventy-six metres across, was completed in 1957, just in time to track the rocket that launched Sputnik, the first artificial satellite. It has since been used to study pulsars, quasars and distant galaxies, and it is part of networks that link telescopes across Britain and the world. It is run by the University of Manchester.",
 x:"From the motorway you can sometimes glimpse the huge white dish of Jodrell Bank.",
 f:"In 2019 Jodrell Bank was added to UNESCO's list of World Heritage Sites.",
 js:"A place in England with a giant dish that listens to space.",
 jl:"In the countryside of Cheshire stands a gigantic white dish, about as wide as a football pitch. It is a radio telescope, which picks up faint radio signals from stars and galaxies instead of light. The place is called Jodrell Bank.",
 jx:"On our school trip to Jodrell Bank we saw the huge dish slowly turn."},
{id:"ast_en_sky_at_night",cat:"ast",only:1,t:"The Sky at Night",
 s:"A long-running BBC television programme about astronomy, presented for decades by Patrick Moore.",
 l:"The Sky at Night was first broadcast in 1957 and has explained eclipses, comets, space missions and new discoveries to generations of viewers. Its first presenter, the astronomer Patrick Moore, known for his monocle, enthusiasm and very fast speech, presented almost every episode until his death in 2012. The programme encouraged many people to become amateur astronomers or space scientists, and it continues with new presenters.",
 x:"Grandma has watched The Sky at Night since she was a little girl.",
 f:"Patrick Moore presented the programme for over fifty-five years, which is often described as the longest run by the same presenter of any television show.",
 js:"A British TV show about stars and space that started a very long time ago.",
 jl:"The Sky at Night is a TV programme that tells people what they can see in the sky and what scientists have discovered in space. It started before your grandparents were grown up, and for many years it was presented by a cheerful astronomer called Patrick Moore.",
 jx:"After watching The Sky at Night, Sophie went outside to look for Mars."},
/* ---------- HISTORY (+6) ---------- */
{id:"his_antiquity",cat:"his",t:"Antiquity",
 s:"The long period of ancient history before the Middle Ages, especially the world of ancient Greece and Rome.",
 l:"Historians often use classical antiquity for the centuries when Greek and Roman civilisations flourished around the Mediterranean, from roughly the eighth century before the common era to the fifth century of the common era. More broadly, antiquity can mean any ancient civilisation, such as Egypt, Mesopotamia, India or China. Many ideas we still use come from this time: democracy, theatre, geometry, Roman law and the alphabet that English is written in.",
 x:"The museum's antiquity gallery has Greek vases and Roman coins.",
 f:"Many historians mark the end of antiquity in the West with the year 476, when the last emperor of the Western Roman Empire was removed from power.",
 js:"The time of ancient peoples, like the Greeks, Romans and Egyptians.",
 jl:"Antiquity means the very old days, thousands of years ago. It is the time of the pyramids in Egypt, the Olympic Games of ancient Greece and the Roman emperors. Many things we still use, like theatre and some letters of our alphabet, began then.",
 jx:"In our lesson about antiquity, we made Roman shields from cardboard."},
{id:"his_crusades",cat:"his",t:"Crusades",
 s:"A series of religious wars, mainly between the eleventh and thirteenth centuries, in which European Christian armies fought to control Jerusalem and other lands.",
 l:"The First Crusade was called by Pope Urban the Second in 1095, and its armies captured Jerusalem in 1099. Over the next two centuries there were further crusades, and the Christian states set up in the eastern Mediterranean were eventually lost. The crusades caused great suffering, including attacks on Muslims, Jews and Eastern Christians, but they also increased trade and the exchange of goods and ideas between Europe and the Middle East. Historians still debate their causes and meaning.",
 x:"The castle we visited in Syria was built by crusaders in the twelfth century.",
 f:"The word crusade comes from the Latin word for cross, because crusaders sewed a cross onto their clothes.",
 js:"Long wars in the Middle Ages, when European armies travelled to fight over Jerusalem.",
 jl:"About a thousand years ago, kings and knights from Europe went on long journeys to the Middle East to fight for the city of Jerusalem, which is holy for Christians, Muslims and Jews. These wars were called the Crusades. Many people suffered, but people also traded and learned from each other.",
 jx:"In the book, the knight wore a red cross on his tunic because he was going on a crusade."},
{id:"his_hanseatic",cat:"his",t:"Hanseatic League",
 s:"A powerful medieval alliance of merchant towns around the Baltic and North Seas that protected and controlled trade.",
 l:"From the thirteenth to the fifteenth century, merchants and towns in northern Europe joined together in the Hanse to protect their ships from pirates, win trading privileges and settle disputes. Lübeck was its leading city, and members included Hamburg, Bremen, Riga, Gdańsk, Toruń and many others. The Hanse ran trading posts as far away as London, Bergen and Novgorod, and it traded fish, grain, timber, furs, wax and cloth. It slowly lost power as strong states and new sea routes emerged.",
 x:"The tall brick warehouses by the harbour date from when the town belonged to the Hanseatic League.",
 f:"Hanse is an old German word for a group or company, and some cities, such as Hamburg and Bremen, still call themselves Hanseatic cities today.",
 js:"A big club of trading towns by the sea in the Middle Ages.",
 jl:"Long ago, merchants in towns by the Baltic Sea and the North Sea made a deal: they would help and protect each other. Their ships carried fish, wood and furs from town to town. This big team of towns was called the Hanseatic League.",
 jx:"We saw an old model of a Hanseatic trading ship in the port museum."},
{id:"his_age_of_discovery",cat:"his",t:"Age of Discovery",
 s:"The period from the fifteenth to the seventeenth century when European sailors explored ocean routes to Africa, Asia and the Americas.",
 l:"Portuguese and Spanish expeditions led the way, helped by better ships, compasses and maps. Christopher Columbus reached the Caribbean in 1492, Vasco da Gama sailed around Africa to India in 1498, and an expedition begun by Ferdinand Magellan completed the first voyage around the world in 1522. These journeys connected continents and changed trade, food and science, but they also brought conquest, deadly diseases and slavery to many peoples. That is why many historians prefer more neutral names, such as the Age of Exploration.",
 x:"The atlas showed the routes of famous voyages from the Age of Discovery.",
 f:"Magellan was killed in the Philippines in 1521, so the first round-the-world voyage was completed by Juan Sebastián Elcano with one surviving ship, the Victoria.",
 js:"The time when European sailors crossed oceans to find new lands and sea routes.",
 jl:"About five hundred years ago, sailors from Europe set off in wooden ships across huge oceans. They wanted to find new ways to trade spices and gold. They reached lands that were new to them, but people already lived there, and for those people the arrival of the Europeans often brought great harm.",
 jx:"For the Age of Discovery project, Ana made a compass out of a cork and a needle."},
{id:"his_berlin_wall",cat:"his",t:"Berlin Wall",
 s:"A guarded wall that divided East and West Berlin from 1961 to 1989 and became a symbol of the Cold War.",
 l:"After the Second World War, Germany was divided, and Berlin was split between the communist East and the democratic West. So many people escaped from East Germany through Berlin that in 1961 its government built a wall, which grew into a heavily guarded border with watchtowers and a strip of open ground. Families and friends were separated for decades. On the ninth of November 1989, after peaceful protests across Eastern Europe, the border crossings were opened and crowds began to break the wall apart. Germany was reunited in 1990.",
 x:"In the museum we touched a real piece of the Berlin Wall covered in graffiti.",
 f:"Pieces of the Berlin Wall now stand in many places around the world, from museums to city parks, as reminders of the divided city.",
 js:"A wall that split the city of Berlin in two for almost thirty years.",
 jl:"For many years, the city of Berlin in Germany had a big wall through the middle. People on one side could not visit friends and family on the other side. In 1989 the gates were opened, and people climbed on the wall, hugged and cheered. Soon the wall was knocked down.",
 jx:"Grandpa remembers watching people dance on the Berlin Wall on television."},
{id:"his_en_great_fire",cat:"his",only:1,t:"Great Fire of London",
 s:"A huge fire that burned through the City of London for several days in September 1666.",
 l:"The fire began in the early hours of the second of September 1666 in the bakery of Thomas Farriner on Pudding Lane. Strong winds, a dry summer and closely packed wooden houses helped it spread, and it burned for about four days. It destroyed around thirteen thousand houses, many churches and the old St Paul's Cathedral. Afterwards, rebuilding used more brick and stone, and Sir Christopher Wren designed the new St Paul's and dozens of city churches.",
 x:"Our class read Samuel Pepys's diary to find out what the Great Fire of London was like.",
 f:"The Monument, a tall stone column near Pudding Lane, was built to remember the fire; its height is said to equal its distance from where the fire started.",
 js:"A giant fire in London in 1666 that burned down much of the city.",
 jl:"More than three hundred and fifty years ago, a fire started in a baker's shop on Pudding Lane in London. The houses were made of wood and stood very close together, so the fire spread quickly. It burned for days. Afterwards, London was rebuilt with more brick and stone.",
 jx:"We built cardboard houses to learn why the Great Fire of London spread so fast."},
/* ---------- MATHEMATICS (+6) ---------- */
{id:"mat_negative_number",cat:"mat",t:"Negative number",
 s:"A number less than zero, written with a minus sign, such as minus five.",
 l:"Negative numbers sit to the left of zero on a number line. We use them for temperatures below freezing, floors below ground level, bank balances in debt and depths below sea level. Adding a negative number is like subtracting, and subtracting a negative number is like adding. When two negative numbers are multiplied together, the answer is positive, which surprises many learners but keeps the rules of arithmetic consistent.",
 x:"Last night the temperature dropped to minus eight degrees.",
 f:"In the seventh century the Indian mathematician Brahmagupta wrote down rules for calculating with negative numbers, which he described as debts, and positive numbers as fortunes.",
 js:"A number below zero, like the temperature on a very cold day.",
 jl:"Count backwards: three, two, one, zero. Can you go further? Yes! The next numbers are minus one, minus two and so on. These are negative numbers. We use them when it is freezing outside or when a lift goes down to the car park underground.",
 jx:"The lift button said minus one, so we went down to the garage."},
{id:"mat_ratio",cat:"mat",t:"Ratio",
 s:"A way of comparing two amounts by showing how many times one contains the other, written like three to one.",
 l:"A ratio tells you the relationship between quantities rather than their exact sizes. If a recipe uses two cups of flour for every one cup of sugar, the ratio is two to one, whether you make a small or a giant cake. Ratios can be simplified like fractions: twelve to eight is the same as three to two. They are used in maps and models, for mixing paint or concrete, in cooking and in comparing prices.",
 x:"The map has a scale ratio of one to fifty thousand.",
 f:"Ratio is a Latin word that meant reckoning or calculation, and it is also the source of the English words reason and ration.",
 js:"A way of comparing two amounts, like two scoops of one thing for each scoop of another.",
 jl:"If you make orange squash with one cup of juice and four cups of water, the ratio is one to four. If you want more squash, you keep the same ratio: two cups of juice and eight cups of water. Then it tastes just the same!",
 jx:"For the slime, we mixed glue and water in a ratio of two to one."},
{id:"mat_angle",cat:"mat",t:"Angle",
 s:"The amount of turn between two lines that meet at a point, usually measured in degrees.",
 l:"A full turn is three hundred and sixty degrees. A right angle, like the corner of a book, is ninety degrees. Angles smaller than a right angle are acute, angles between ninety and one hundred and eighty degrees are obtuse, and a straight line makes an angle of one hundred and eighty degrees. Angles are measured with a protractor. The three angles inside any flat triangle always add up to one hundred and eighty degrees.",
 x:"The ramp rises at an angle of only five degrees, so wheelchairs can use it easily.",
 f:"Dividing a full turn into three hundred and sixty degrees is usually traced back to ancient Babylonian astronomers, who counted in sixties.",
 js:"How much two lines open up from the point where they meet.",
 jl:"Open a book a little: the two covers make a small angle. Open it wider, and the angle gets bigger. The corner of a square is a special angle called a right angle. We measure angles in degrees with a see-through half circle called a protractor.",
 jx:"Kai measured the angle of his skateboard ramp with a protractor."},
{id:"mat_area_perimeter",cat:"mat",t:"Area and perimeter",
 s:"Area is the amount of flat surface a shape covers; perimeter is the total length around its edge.",
 l:"Perimeter is measured in units of length, such as metres, and you find it by adding up the lengths of all the sides. Area is measured in square units, such as square metres, and for a rectangle you find it by multiplying length by width. The two are easy to confuse but behave differently: two fields can have the same perimeter but very different areas. You need perimeter to buy a fence and area to buy a carpet.",
 x:"To work out how much paint we need, we first calculated the area of the wall.",
 f:"Of all shapes with the same perimeter, the circle encloses the largest area.",
 js:"Area is how much space is inside a shape; perimeter is the distance all the way around it.",
 jl:"Imagine a garden. If you walk all the way around its edge, the distance you walk is the perimeter. If you cover the whole garden with grass squares, the number of squares you need is the area. A fence is about perimeter; a carpet is about area.",
 jx:"We used string to measure the perimeter of the classroom table."},
{id:"mat_polygon",cat:"mat",t:"Polygon",
 s:"A flat shape made of straight sides joined end to end to form a closed figure.",
 l:"Triangles, squares, pentagons and hexagons are all polygons. They are named after their number of sides: a pentagon has five, a hexagon six and an octagon eight. A regular polygon has all sides and all angles equal, like a stop sign. Circles are not polygons because they have no straight sides. Polygons are everywhere, from floor tiles and road signs to the tiny triangles that make up shapes in computer games.",
 x:"The football is covered in black pentagons and white hexagons, all of them polygons.",
 f:"The word comes from Greek and means many angles; bees build hexagonal cells because hexagons fit together without gaps and use little wax.",
 js:"A flat shape with straight sides, like a triangle or a square.",
 jl:"A polygon is any closed shape made only of straight lines. A triangle has three sides, a square has four, and a hexagon, like the cells in a beehive, has six. A circle is not a polygon, because it is round and has no straight sides.",
 jx:"Nina drew polygons on squared paper and coloured each one differently."},
{id:"mat_rounding",cat:"mat",t:"Rounding",
 s:"Replacing a number with a simpler one that is close to it, such as to the nearest ten.",
 l:"We round numbers to make them easier to say, remember or estimate with. To round to the nearest ten, look at the units digit: if it is five or more, round up; if it is less than five, round down. So forty-seven becomes fifty, and forty-three becomes forty. Prices, distances and measurements are often rounded. It is useful for quick mental checks, but small rounding errors can build up in long calculations.",
 x:"The bill was nineteen pounds eighty, so I rounded it to twenty in my head.",
 f:"Computers and accountants sometimes use a rule called round half to even, so that a value exactly halfway, like two and a half, is rounded to the nearest even number, which avoids always pushing totals upwards.",
 js:"Changing a number to a nearby easier number, like 49 to 50.",
 jl:"If something costs ninety-eight pence, you can say it costs about one pound. That is rounding: choosing a nice, easy number that is very close to the real one. It helps you guess answers quickly in your head.",
 jx:"There were 48 children at the party, so we rounded it to about 50 when buying cups."},
/* ---------- ARCHAEOLOGY (+6) ---------- */
{id:"arc_tell",cat:"arc",t:"Tell",
 s:"An artificial hill formed by the remains of a settlement that was rebuilt on the same spot over many centuries.",
 l:"In parts of the Middle East and south-east Europe, houses were often built of mud brick. When old houses collapsed, people flattened the rubble and built new homes on top, so the ground slowly rose. After thousands of years, this created a mound full of layers, each from a different period. Archaeologists dig into tells to read this history from top to bottom, with the oldest layers at the base. Tells are common in Turkey, Syria, Iraq, Israel and the Balkans.",
 x:"The village sits next to a tell where people lived for almost three thousand years.",
 f:"The site of ancient Troy, at Hisarlik in Turkey, is a tell with many layers of cities built one on top of another.",
 js:"A hill made by people building new houses on top of old ones for a very long time.",
 jl:"Imagine building a sandcastle town, then building a new one on top, and again and again. After a long time you would have a little hill. In some countries people did this with real mud-brick houses for thousands of years. These hills are called tells.",
 jx:"The guide said the hill under our feet was a tell full of ancient homes."},
{id:"arc_midden",cat:"arc",t:"Midden",
 s:"An old rubbish heap, which archaeologists study to learn what people ate, used and threw away.",
 l:"Middens may contain shells, animal bones, broken pottery, ash, tools and food waste. Because they built up over time, they often have clear layers that show how life changed. Shell middens along coasts can be enormous, made from millions of shells left by generations of people. Rubbish tells honest stories: it shows the everyday diet and habits of ordinary people, who rarely appear in written records.",
 x:"The team sieved soil from the midden and found fish bones and a bone comb.",
 f:"The word midden comes from an old Scandinavian word for a dung heap.",
 js:"A very old rubbish pile that helps archaeologists learn about the past.",
 jl:"Think about what your family throws away in a week: food peels, broken things, bottles. Long ago people also had rubbish heaps. Archaeologists love them, because the old shells, bones and broken pots show what people ate and used every day.",
 jx:"Our teacher joked that one day archaeologists will study our school bins like a midden."},
{id:"arc_knapping",cat:"arc",t:"Flint knapping",
 s:"Shaping stone such as flint or obsidian into tools by striking off flakes.",
 l:"Flint and similar stones break with smooth, curved fractures that leave very sharp edges. Early people struck a core with a hammerstone, antler or bone to remove flakes, then used or refined them into knives, scrapers, axes and arrowheads. Waste flakes, called debitage, are common finds and show where tools were made. Experimental archaeologists practise knapping today to understand ancient techniques, though it needs eye protection because flakes fly off at speed.",
 x:"At the museum workshop, a craftsman showed how flint knapping makes a blade in minutes.",
 f:"A freshly knapped obsidian edge can be thinner than the edge of a steel scalpel.",
 js:"Hitting stones carefully to make sharp tools, like people did in the Stone Age.",
 jl:"In the Stone Age there was no metal. People made knives and arrowheads by tapping one stone against another so that sharp pieces flaked off. This skill is called knapping. Today experts still practise it to learn how our ancestors did it.",
 jx:"We watched a video of flint knapping and saw a stone turn into an arrowhead."},
{id:"arc_amphora",cat:"arc",t:"Amphora",
 s:"A tall clay jar with two handles and a narrow neck, used in the ancient Mediterranean to transport wine, olive oil and other goods.",
 l:"Amphorae were the shipping containers of the ancient world. Their pointed bottoms could be pushed into sand or stacked in layers in a ship's hold. Different regions made different shapes, and many carry stamps or painted labels, so archaeologists can tell where an amphora was made and what it carried. Shipwrecks full of amphorae help map ancient trade routes across the Mediterranean.",
 x:"Divers found dozens of amphorae lying on the seabed beside the wreck.",
 f:"Monte Testaccio in Rome is an artificial hill made almost entirely of broken Roman amphorae that once held olive oil.",
 js:"A big clay jar with two handles, used long ago to carry olive oil and wine.",
 jl:"In ancient Greece and Rome there were no plastic bottles or cardboard boxes. People carried oil, wine and fish sauce in tall clay jars with two handles and a pointy bottom. These jars are called amphorae. Ships carried hundreds of them at once.",
 jx:"Ella made a paper amphora and painted Greek patterns on it."},
{id:"arc_tutankhamun",cat:"arc",t:"Tutankhamun's tomb",
 s:"The burial place of a young Egyptian pharaoh, discovered nearly intact in the Valley of the Kings in 1922.",
 l:"Tutankhamun ruled Egypt for about ten years in the fourteenth century before the common era and died young. His small tomb was hidden under later building debris, so it escaped most of the robbing that emptied other royal tombs. In November 1922 the archaeologist Howard Carter, funded by Lord Carnarvon, found its entrance. Inside were gilded shrines, chariots, jewellery, furniture and the famous gold mask. Recording and removing the objects carefully took about ten years.",
 x:"The gold mask from Tutankhamun's tomb is one of the most famous objects in the world.",
 f:"The tomb held more than five thousand objects, from golden thrones to baskets of fruit and sandals.",
 js:"The treasure-filled tomb of a young Egyptian king, found about a hundred years ago.",
 jl:"Tutankhamun was a king of ancient Egypt who became pharaoh when he was only a child. More than three thousand years later, an archaeologist found his tomb, still full of treasure: golden furniture, jewels and a beautiful gold mask.",
 jx:"Sara built a Lego model of Tutankhamun's tomb with a golden mask inside."},
{id:"arc_fieldwalking",cat:"arc",t:"Fieldwalking",
 s:"A survey method in which archaeologists walk across a ploughed field in lines, collecting and recording objects lying on the surface.",
 l:"Ploughing brings pottery, flint, tiles and other small finds up from buried sites. In fieldwalking, a team walks slowly along evenly spaced lines and bags each find, noting exactly where it was picked up. When the finds are mapped, clusters can reveal hidden settlements, workshops or roads without any digging. It is cheap, needs little equipment and is often done with volunteers, but it must have the landowner's permission.",
 x:"After the autumn ploughing, the local history group went fieldwalking and found Roman pottery.",
 f:"Fieldwalking works best after rain, which washes the soil off finds so that pottery and flint stand out on the surface.",
 js:"Walking slowly across a field to look for old things lying on the ground.",
 jl:"When a farmer ploughs a field, old bits of pottery and stone tools can come up to the surface. Archaeologists walk across the field in straight lines, picking up what they see and noting where it was. Then they can guess where people lived long ago.",
 jx:"We went fieldwalking with the museum and found a piece of a very old pot."},
/* ---------- COOKING (+6) ---------- */
{id:"cook_kneading",cat:"cook",t:"Kneading",
 s:"Working dough by pressing, folding and stretching it, so that it becomes smooth and stretchy.",
 l:"Kneading develops gluten, the network of proteins in wheat flour that traps gas and helps bread rise and keep its shape. By hand, you push the dough away with the heel of your hand, fold it back and turn it, repeating for several minutes. At first the dough is rough and sticky, then it becomes smooth and springy. Cakes and pastry need little or no kneading, because a strong gluten network would make them tough.",
 x:"After ten minutes of kneading, the dough was smooth and bounced back when I poked it.",
 f:"Some breads need almost no kneading: a wet dough left to rest for many hours develops gluten slowly on its own.",
 js:"Pushing and folding dough with your hands to make bread.",
 jl:"To make bread, you squash the dough with your hands, fold it over, turn it and squash it again. This is kneading. It is a bit like playing with play dough. It makes the dough stretchy, so the bread can rise up tall and fluffy.",
 jx:"Kneading the pizza dough was my favourite part of the cooking class."},
{id:"cook_poaching",cat:"cook",t:"Poaching",
 s:"Cooking food gently in liquid that is hot but well below boiling.",
 l:"In poaching, the liquid barely moves, with only occasional small bubbles. This gentle heat suits delicate foods that would break apart or toughen in boiling water, such as eggs, fish, chicken breast and fruit. The liquid can be water, stock, milk or wine, and it may be flavoured with herbs or spices. Poaching uses no fat, so it is a light way of cooking, and the liquid can often be turned into a sauce afterwards.",
 x:"For breakfast he poached two eggs and served them on toast.",
 f:"In cooking, poach comes from an old French word for a bag or pocket, perhaps because the egg white wraps around the yolk like a little pouch.",
 js:"Cooking food gently in hot water that isn't bubbling hard.",
 jl:"Some foods are delicate, like eggs or fish. If you cook them in wildly bubbling water, they fall apart. Instead, you can put them in water that is very hot but only gently steaming. That is called poaching.",
 jx:"Mum poached pears in apple juice for a warm pudding."},
{id:"cook_steaming",cat:"cook",t:"Steaming",
 s:"Cooking food in the hot vapour rising from boiling water, without the food touching the water.",
 l:"Food is placed in a basket or on a rack above simmering water, with a lid on top. Steaming is gentle and keeps food moist, and vegetables lose fewer vitamins than when boiled, because less leaks into the water. It is central to many cuisines: Chinese dumplings and buns are cooked in stacked bamboo baskets, and puddings are traditionally steamed in Britain. Care is needed when lifting the lid, as steam can burn.",
 x:"We steamed the broccoli for four minutes so it stayed bright green.",
 f:"Steam can burn worse than boiling water at the same temperature, because it releases a lot of extra heat when it turns back into water on your skin.",
 js:"Cooking food in the hot cloud that comes off boiling water.",
 jl:"When water boils, it makes a hot cloud called steam. If you put food in a basket above the water and close the lid, the steam cooks it. Steamed vegetables stay bright and crunchy. Always let a grown-up lift the lid, because steam is very hot.",
 jx:"At the restaurant, the dumplings came in a little bamboo steaming basket."},
{id:"cook_zest",cat:"cook",t:"Zest",
 s:"The thin, coloured outer layer of citrus peel, grated or peeled to add flavour.",
 l:"The zest of lemons, limes and oranges contains tiny pockets of fragrant oil, which give a strong fresh taste to cakes, dressings, marinades and drinks. Only the coloured skin is used: the white layer underneath, called the pith, is bitter. Zest is taken off with a fine grater, a special zester or a peeler. If you plan to use zest, it is good to wash the fruit well, and many cooks choose unwaxed fruit.",
 x:"She added the zest of one lemon to the muffin batter.",
 f:"A popular kitchen grater for zest started life as a woodworking tool for smoothing wood before cooks discovered it in the nineteen nineties.",
 js:"The thin, colourful skin of a lemon or orange, grated to add flavour.",
 jl:"Rub a lemon's skin and smell your fingers: it smells amazing! That smell comes from oil in the thin, yellow outside layer. Cooks grate this layer, called zest, into cakes and sauces. They leave the white part underneath, because it tastes bitter.",
 jx:"I grated orange zest over the pancakes, and the whole kitchen smelled lovely."},
{id:"cook_baking_blind",cat:"cook",t:"Baking blind",
 s:"Baking a pastry case on its own, before the filling is added.",
 l:"Wet fillings, such as custard or quiche mixture, can make pastry soggy if both go into the oven raw. To prevent this, the pastry case is lined with baking paper, filled with ceramic baking beans, dried beans or rice to stop it puffing up or sliding down the sides, and baked until set. The weights and paper are then removed and the pastry is often baked a little longer until dry and lightly golden, before filling.",
 x:"The recipe tells you to bake the tart case blind for fifteen minutes before adding the lemon filling.",
 f:"The dried beans used for baking blind cannot be eaten afterwards, but they can be cooled and used again and again for the same job.",
 js:"Baking a pastry case empty before you put the filling in.",
 jl:"Some pies have runny fillings that would make the pastry soft and soggy. So bakers first bake the empty pastry case, weighed down with dried beans so it keeps its shape. Afterwards, they pour in the filling. This trick is called baking blind.",
 jx:"Grandma showed me how to bake the tart case blind with a jar of old beans."},
{id:"cook_en_fish_and_chips",cat:"cook",only:1,t:"Fish and chips",
 s:"A classic British takeaway of battered fried fish, usually cod or haddock, served with thick-cut fried potatoes.",
 l:"Fish and chips became popular in British towns in the second half of the nineteenth century, helped by railways that brought fresh fish inland from the ports. The fish is dipped in batter and deep-fried, and the chips are thicker than French fries. Traditional extras include salt, malt vinegar, mushy peas, a pickled onion or curry sauce. Many families have a fish and chip supper on Fridays, and a chippy can be found in almost every British town.",
 x:"On the last day of the holiday we ate fish and chips on the seafront.",
 f:"During the Second World War, fish and chips were not rationed in Britain, which helped keep them a popular and affordable meal.",
 js:"Fried fish in crunchy batter with thick chips, a favourite British meal.",
 jl:"Fish and chips is one of Britain's best-loved meals. The fish is covered in batter and fried until golden and crunchy, and the chips are thick and soft inside. People often eat them out of the paper by the seaside, with salt and vinegar.",
 jx:"After swimming, we shared fish and chips and fought off the seagulls."},
/* ---------- ART (+6) ---------- */
{id:"art_landscape",cat:"art",t:"Landscape painting",
 s:"A picture whose main subject is natural scenery, such as mountains, fields, rivers or the sky.",
 l:"For centuries landscape was mostly a background in paintings of religious or historical stories. In the seventeenth century, Dutch painters made it a subject of its own, with wide skies and flat countryside. Later artists such as John Constable and J. M. W. Turner in England, and the Impressionists in France, painted outdoors to capture changing light and weather. A landscape can be realistic, dreamlike or almost abstract, and it often shows a mood as much as a place.",
 x:"The gallery's landscape room is full of stormy seas and misty mountains.",
 f:"The English word landscape was borrowed from the Dutch word landschap, around the time Dutch landscape painting was becoming famous.",
 js:"A picture of nature, like hills, trees, rivers and sky.",
 jl:"A landscape painting shows a view of the outdoors: maybe a forest, a beach, mountains or a field full of flowers. Some artists carry their paints outside and paint what they see, while the light and the clouds change.",
 jx:"For homework, Theo painted a landscape of the park with a big orange sunset."},
{id:"art_sketch",cat:"art",t:"Sketch",
 s:"A quick, rough drawing that captures the main idea or shape of something.",
 l:"Artists sketch to explore ideas, practise, record what they see and plan bigger works. A sketch is usually made with pencil, charcoal or pen, without worrying about details or perfect lines. Sketchbooks of famous artists are treasured because they show how ideas developed. Designers, architects and engineers sketch too, and in a wider sense a sketch can mean any short, rough version of something, such as a short comic scene in a comedy show.",
 x:"Before painting the mural, she filled three pages with sketches.",
 f:"The word came into English through Dutch from the Italian word schizzo, meaning a splash or a quick drawing.",
 js:"A quick drawing that doesn't have to be perfect.",
 jl:"A sketch is a fast drawing that shows the main shapes of something. You don't need to rub out mistakes or add every detail. Artists make lots of sketches to try out ideas before they start a big picture.",
 jx:"Ava keeps a sketchbook in her bag and draws the dogs she sees in the park."},
{id:"art_sculpture",cat:"art",t:"Sculpture",
 s:"Art made in three dimensions, by carving, modelling, casting or assembling materials.",
 l:"Sculptors work in many ways. They can carve away stone or wood, model soft clay or wax, cast metal such as bronze in a mould, or join found objects together. A free-standing sculpture can be walked around and seen from every side, while a relief stands out from a flat background, like the picture on a coin. Modern sculpture also includes installations, moving pieces and works made of light, sound or even ice.",
 x:"In the park there is a bronze sculpture of a girl reading a book.",
 f:"Many bronze sculptures are made by lost-wax casting: a wax model is covered in a mould, the wax is melted out, and molten bronze is poured into the space it leaves.",
 js:"Art you can walk around, like a statue made of stone, clay or metal.",
 jl:"A painting is flat, but a sculpture has height, width and depth, just like a real object. Sculptors carve stone, shape clay with their hands or pour hot metal into moulds. You can walk around a sculpture and see it from every side.",
 jx:"We made sculptures of our pets out of clay and baked them in the school kiln."},
{id:"art_baroque",cat:"art",t:"Baroque",
 s:"A dramatic, richly decorated style of art, architecture and music from the seventeenth and early eighteenth centuries.",
 l:"Baroque art tries to move the viewer's feelings. Paintings use strong light and shadow, swirling movement and intense moments, and churches and palaces are full of gold, curves, painted ceilings and sculptures that seem to burst out of the walls. The style began in Rome and spread across Catholic Europe and beyond. Famous Baroque artists include Caravaggio, Bernini and Rubens, and in music the period includes Bach, Vivaldi and Handel.",
 x:"The ceiling of the Baroque church was painted so that the sky seems to open above you.",
 f:"Baroque is often traced to a Portuguese word for an irregularly shaped pearl, and it was first used as an insult for art that seemed too strange and extravagant.",
 js:"A very fancy, dramatic style from about four hundred years ago, with lots of gold and swirls.",
 jl:"Baroque art is like a big show. Churches and palaces in this style have golden decorations, curly shapes and ceilings painted with clouds and flying figures. Paintings are full of movement and strong light, as if something exciting is happening right now.",
 jx:"Inside the Baroque palace, every room had gold mirrors and painted ceilings."},
{id:"art_pop_art",cat:"art",t:"Pop art",
 s:"An art movement of the nineteen fifties and sixties that used images from advertising, comics and everyday products.",
 l:"Pop art began in Britain and the United States as a reaction against serious, abstract art. Artists took images from popular culture, such as soup cans, film stars, cartoons and adverts, and presented them in galleries, often with bright flat colours and techniques borrowed from printing. Some celebrated modern consumer life and some gently mocked it. Well-known pop artists include Andy Warhol, Roy Lichtenstein and, in Britain, Richard Hamilton and Peter Blake.",
 x:"Her bedroom poster looks like pop art, with bright dots and a speech bubble.",
 f:"In 1962 Andy Warhol showed thirty-two paintings of Campbell's soup cans, one for each flavour the company sold at the time.",
 js:"Bright, bold art that uses pictures from comics, adverts and shop products.",
 jl:"Pop artists looked at things everyone knew, like cartoons, sweets wrappers and tins of soup, and turned them into art. Their pictures are often bright, with bold colours and black outlines, a bit like a giant comic strip.",
 jx:"In art class we made pop art pictures of our favourite snacks."},
{id:"art_en_royal_academy",cat:"art",only:1,t:"Royal Academy of Arts",
 s:"An institution in London, founded in 1768, that runs an art school and holds major exhibitions.",
 l:"The Royal Academy was founded under King George the Third, with the painter Joshua Reynolds as its first president. It is run by artists and architects, known as Royal Academicians, and it has its home at Burlington House on Piccadilly. It runs the Royal Academy Schools, a free postgraduate art school, and organises important exhibitions. Its most famous event is the Summer Exhibition, where anyone can submit work to be shown beside that of established artists.",
 x:"My aunt was thrilled when her etching was chosen for the Royal Academy's Summer Exhibition.",
 f:"The Summer Exhibition has been held every year since 1769, making it one of the longest-running open art exhibitions in the world.",
 js:"A famous art school and gallery in London.",
 jl:"The Royal Academy of Arts is a grand building in London where artists study and show their work. Every summer it holds a big exhibition, and anyone can send in a picture. Famous artists and complete beginners can end up hanging on the same walls.",
 jx:"We visited the Royal Academy and saw paintings from all kinds of artists."}
]);
